(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ocular-trauma-vignettes"]={"title":"Ocular Trauma Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a foreign body sensation after a day of metalwork. Fluorescein shows several fine vertical lines running down the cornea. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Patch the eye and review in one week","Refer for urgent surgical exploration","Evert the upper eyelid","Examine the lower fornix with a cotton bud"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vertical linear defects mean the object is under the upper lid, scraping with each blink. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which symptom indicates the macula has become involved in a retinal detachment?","choices":["The eye has become painful","Central visual acuity has dropped","Floaters have increased","Flashes have become more frequent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Field defects come first; central acuity falls with macular involvement. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 36-year-old has a corneal laceration with a cut lens capsule and an opaque, swollen lens. When is lensectomy usually performed?","choices":["At the same time as the globe repair, always","Only if vision fails to improve after a year","Never, since the lens will clear on its own","Later, once hyphema and inflammation settle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is required but often deferred from the globe repair. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 34-year-old machinist has photophobia, tearing and a foreign body sensation after a day at the lathe. Diffuse light shows nothing. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure and review in one week","Patch the eye and discharge","Prescribe topical anaesthetic for comfort","Slit lamp examination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Larger objects show on diffuse light; smaller ones need the slit lamp. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 48-year-old has a deep laceration in the medial third of the lower lid. What is the consequence if it is not repaired properly?","choices":["Chronic tearing","Permanent ptosis","Loss of central vision","Corneal ulceration within days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The canalicular injury is what makes the medial third special. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A cycloplegic drop is prescribed for a patient with a hyphema. What is it doing?","choices":["Lowering the intraocular pressure directly","Dissolving the clot","Constricting the pupil to trap the blood","Paralysing the ciliary body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atropine, homatropine and scopolamine are used this way. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 52-year-old is brought in after a road traffic collision with an obviously injured left eye, confusion and shallow breathing. Which is the most appropriate first action?","choices":["Order computed tomography of the orbits","Assess airway, breathing and circulation","Measure visual acuity in both eyes","Instil fluorescein and examine the cornea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The eye is assessed after the patient is stabilised. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 55-year-old with a fresh retinal detachment asks how urgently she needs to be seen. Which is the most accurate response?","choices":["Only if the curtain spreads further","Immediately, but only if the other eye is affected too","By an ophthalmologist within 24 hours","At the next available clinic within a month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The referral is immediate. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 59-year-old describes flashes for two weeks, then a curtain yesterday. Which sequence explains this?","choices":["Retinal break first, then vitreous liquefaction","Choroidal neovascularisation with haemorrhage","Optic nerve inflammation","Vitreous detachment, then a retinal break"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is the usual order of events. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man was struck in the right eye by a baseball. The lid is swollen, the conjunctiva is deeply haemorrhagic and the globe feels soft. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Shield the eye and call ophthalmology","Measure the intraocular pressure","Instil a mydriatic and examine the fundus","Irrigate the eye with copious saline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A soft eye with massive haemorrhagic chemosis after blunt force is a globe rupture until proven otherwise. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 28-year-old has a hyphema and a distorted pupil after a penetrating injury. Which step must be omitted?","choices":["Calling ophthalmology","Measuring the pressure","Placing a rigid shield","Giving an antiemetic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pressing on a possibly open globe risks extruding its contents. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 68-year-old on apixaban develops painful proptosis of one eye over an hour with no injury. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Periorbital haematoma","Orbital cellulitis","Thyroid eye disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is not always traumatic, and anticoagulants are a listed cause. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A patient's optic nerve was damaged at the moment of an orbital injury. What should be expected from surgery?","choices":["Surgery reliably restores the lost vision","Surgery restores vision if done within 24 hours","Vision recovers spontaneously in most cases","It is unlikely to improve, and surgery may worsen it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is why the decision is made carefully. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 26-year-old was punched in the eye. He has periorbital swelling, numbness of the cheek, and double vision when looking upward. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Blowout fracture with inferior rectus entrapment","Third nerve palsy","Traumatic optic neuropathy","Orbital cellulitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Infraorbital numbness with upward gaze diplopia is the combination. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A conjunctival foreign body is visible on diffuse light examination in a 28-year-old. Which is the most appropriate removal method?","choices":["Referral for operative removal","A cotton-tipped applicator","A sterile 27-gauge needle","A battery-operated burr"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A conjunctival object lifts off with a swab. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 55-year-old has a confirmed globe rupture. Which is the definitive management?","choices":["Topical antibiotics and observation","Intravitreal antibiotics alone","Immediate surgical repair","Pressure patching and review in 24 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Everything else is holding measures until then. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 47-year-old struck by a metal shard has an eye in which the pupil is drawn to one side toward a corneal wound and the anterior chamber looks flat. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Open globe injury","Corneal abrasion","Anterior uveitis","Acute angle closure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pupil pulled toward the wound with a flat chamber is the picture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A conscious and cooperative 33-year-old has a periorbital laceration after an assault. Which examination elements are appropriate?","choices":["Gonioscopy of the drainage angle","Corneal sensation testing alone","Inspection, pupils, acuity and fields","Dilated fundoscopy first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, done without causing further damage. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 44-year-old has clear rhinorrhoea after a fall. Which two bedside findings would support cerebrospinal fluid rather than nasal secretions?","choices":["A positive dextrose stick and a halo sign","A negative dextrose stick and clear fluid","Purulent colour and a foul smell","Fluorescein uptake and photophobia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Those are the two bedside tests described. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 30-year-old with a hyphema asks how long the dangerous period lasts. Which is the most accurate response?","choices":["The first 24 hours only","There is no dangerous period once bleeding stops","The first month, evenly throughout","The first 72 hours, when most rebleeding happens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A secondary haemorrhage is what costs vision permanently. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 15-year-old with sickle cell disease sustains a hyphema. Why does that history matter?","choices":["It makes aminocaproic acid unnecessary","It means the pressure need not be checked","It raises the risk of complications","It rules out a rebleed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sickle cell disease is specifically flagged. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which combination of findings should raise a basilar skull fracture after head trauma?","choices":["Raccoon eyes, Battle sign and haemotympanum","Ptosis, a dilated pupil and diplopia","A red eye with discharge and lid crusting","Diplopia on upward gaze with cheek numbness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Those are the indirect signs. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 32-year-old has an orbital floor fracture with no entrapment, no globe injury and a clear maxillary sinus. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Antibiotics and admission","Neurosurgical consult","Immediate ophthalmology consult for a true blowout","Ice and analgesia, with follow-up in 2 to 3 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is the pathway when nothing else is involved. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 44-year-old has an orbital floor fracture with no entrapment and no globe injury, and blood is seen in the maxillary sinus. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Neurosurgical admission","Ice, analgesia and antibiotics","Immediate operative repair","Ice and analgesia alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blood in the sinus is what adds the antibiotic. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 29-year-old has a wooden splinter driven into the periorbital tissue while gardening. Beyond removing nothing and imaging appropriately, which additional step is required?","choices":["Confirm tetanus is up to date","Start systemic antivirals","Give a mydriatic to examine the retina","Begin oral corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Penetration with metal or organic material carries the risk. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A patient with a suspected basilar skull fracture has clear fluid dripping from the nose that forms a double ring on the pillowcase. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Neurosurgery consult and admission","Nasal packing to stop the drainage","Ophthalmology consult","Prophylactic antibiotics alone, then discharge"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A cerebrospinal fluid leak forces both. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 50-year-old has a laceration involving the tarsal plate of the upper lid. Which is the most appropriate action?","choices":["Leave it open for 24 hours then close it","Refer routinely in 2 to 3 weeks","Consult ophthalmology","Repair it with absorbable sutures in the department"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tarsal plate involvement is an explicit referral criterion. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 39-year-old fell from a ladder. She has bruising around both eyes, bruising behind one ear, and blood behind the eardrum. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Periorbital cellulitis","Basilar skull fracture","Bilateral orbital floor fractures","Nasal fracture with periorbital spread"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Raccoon eyes, Battle sign and haemotympanum together. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 12-year-old scratched his cornea playing. His parent asks for anaesthetic drops for the pain at home. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Prescribe a corticosteroid drop instead","Prescribe nothing at all","Decline, because they delay healing and can cause an ulcer","Prescribe them for 24 hours only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This holds at any age. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman scratched her cornea removing a contact lens. Fluorescein confirms an abrasion. She asks for something for the pain to use at home. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["No treatment, since abrasions heal regardless","Analgesia and a topical antibiotic","Topical anaesthetic drops four times daily","Topical corticosteroid drops for comfort"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Take-home anaesthetic delays healing, masks worsening symptoms and can cause a corneal ulcer. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"}]};
